WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:14.100
Juan “Hungrybox” Debiedma has always lived in the shadows.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.180
He lived in the shadow of his father, who never supported him.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.440
He lived in the shadow of the Smash community, who turned him into a villain.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:35.860
He lived in the shadow of other players, who were always just ahead of him both in terms

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:38.860
of gameplay and tournament placings.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:48.380
But through it all, he never gave up on being the best and proving everyone wrong.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.020
He never gave up on himself, or on Smash.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.600
This is his story.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:08.220
Hungrybox was born on June 21, 1993 in Buenos Aires, Argentina.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.460
But he didn’t stay there for long.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:16.369
He was the youngest of three brothers, and his parents, Juan Daniel and Lucia, lived

00:01:16.369 --> 00:01:19.540
a somewhat charmed life in their home country.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:26.000
But much of that came crashing down in 1994, when Argentina was hit by a recession after

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.360
Mexico devalued its peso.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.400
Banks collapsing and investors pulling out of the country’s developing business, the

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:38.700
Debiedma family made their way to Orlando, Florida in search of a brighter future.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.840
Much of the family fit in fine, but Juan Daniel didn’t.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:48.100
By all accounts, he didn’t love the change in lifestyle, and that triggered his temper.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:52.060
Juan Daniel grew angrier, and became obsessed with money.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:58.000
He spent money, ran out of money, then went back to Argentina to try it all over again.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
By 2009, he was ready to leave his family again, this time for the last time.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:10.300
"For most kids, even for me there definitely was a lot of rough stuff. But honestly,

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:14.940
when he left for the very last time, it was just me my brothers and my mom, it was like the biggest relief

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:21.080
in the world. It was just like, I can go home. And I can do my homework now without the sounds of huge

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:24.980
yelling in the background. I can like just chill and know I don't have to worry about anything anymore."

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.240
"It was a lot more ton of focus on just me."

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.200
Without Juan Daniel in their life, the young Debiedma brothers were supported by their

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.320
mother, Lucia, who Hungrybox credits with just about everything.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:42.560
"I definitely spent a lot more time with my mom. Because I didn't want her to be lonely I guess. And

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.600
From that I guess we have built a really strong mother son relationship.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.520
So like we're really close today."

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.400
"People say they're dealt different hands in life, somje kids are born with a silver spoon in their mouth

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.520
Some kids are born being an heir to huge fortune or royalty. I was born with the best mom in the world

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.480
so like I owe her everything."

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:06.800
As his home life became a little more stable, Hbox began to explore the world of competitive Melee.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:12.680
And his first tournament, a monthly at Gigabits LAN center in Florida, inspired him to make

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.680
his Jigglypuff into the best in the world.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:21.800
"My first real tournament was Gigabits 2007. Near UCF, it was a LAN centre."

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:27.780
"And they had a first official legit tournament with 2 out of 3s, a bracket system, low tier tournaments.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.880
Man the rush, I still remember to this day the rush of walking in the rush of seeing top players compete

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.480
It's unlike anything else, it's such an amazing experience for me. I was hooked from the first day I walked in."

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:44.320
Hungrybox placed ninth there, an impressive result for his first real tournament, but

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.519
it just inspired him to grind.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:51.280
"I was playing every day with my friends. There was this group of players called WATO (What Are The Odds)

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:56.480
in the Orlando area, and they kind of took me in as their underling and taught me all the matchups

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:58.800
and I played with them every day and I would host them at my house.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.800
My parents house, every friday we'd have these things called "box fests"

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:08.080
In my you know, cramped bedroom that the AC was not working so it was like 80 degrees in there

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:14.760
It was just funny cause, those moments are really what molded my understanding of matchups.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:19.600
In those early days, Melee’s competitive scene was disorganised and scattered.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.860
Hungrybox wasn’t part of Melee’s first golden age, when the game was played on MLG’s

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:30.240
circuit and players like Ken, PC Chris and Isai ruled the scene.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.960
Instead, he rose to prominence in the dark ages of Melee.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:41.060
And while he made his national debut at Revival of Melee, Hungrybox credits GENESIS as the

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:43.640
tournament that jump started his pro career.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:49.520
Genesis was the biggest Melee tournament at the time, and it attracted America’s finest.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:54.060
Most eyes were on Mang0, who was considered one of the best Melee players in the world

00:04:54.060 --> 00:05:00.220
at the time, and Armada, Europe’s top Smasher who came to make a name for himself in NA.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:05.660
But flying under everyone’s radar was Hungrybox, who tore through the bracket, losing only

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:06.920
to Mang0.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:13.140
Later that year, Hungrybox attended Revival of Melee 2 and finally made his name truly

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:13.680
known.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:31.280
(Gameplay)

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:37.100
Following a slew of second place finishes, Apex 2010 the following year marked his next

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.500
national victory.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:42.590
But he wasn’t consistently dominating the competition when it counted.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:48.960
In fact, he didn’t see another major first place finish again until 2013.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:06:27.060
(Casting)

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:33.560
Hungrybox learned Melee on Jigglypuff, who, at first glance, doesn’t exactly seem like

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:36.220
one of the game’s more potent characters.

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:41.740
Jigglypuff has been in Smash since the franchise’s inception, but was put in the game as something

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:42.860
of a joke.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:48.310
She’s slow, doesn’t do much damage, and most of her special moves have severe downsides

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:49.500
if you miss them.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:51.780
But Hungrybox was drawn to her.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:56.810
As a kid, his hobby was trying to beat everything in Melee’s single player modes, and as he

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:02.560
futilely tried to beat All-Star mode without dying, he discovered Puff’s Rest move.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:06.940
"I was pushing like 20 trophies or whatever, one of them was beating all-star mode on very hard

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:13.720
without dying, or so I thought. I tried doing it, kept losing kept losing, I'm like, "

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:19.080
which character in this game can restore HP?" I'm like, "Wait a minute Jigglypuff can do that, because she has

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.180
rest. I'm pretty sure in Pokemon that restores HP."

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:28.080
And I remember I used it for the first time, and it's just this useless f*cking move. Does 0%. You're just

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.320
stuck there. I'm like, "she's the worst character in the game."

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:33.760
An hour later, I'll tell this to everyone.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.040
Had this not happened I wouldn't be here today.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:40.320
I was walking down the hall to the bathroom, I turned to the right, my brother's room.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:46.160
He happened to be playing Smash 64, and right as I look, he landed a rest with puff in that game.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:49.580
And I looked at it and I was like "Woah, how'd you do that?"

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:51.580
He's like "Yeah she just has it, down b."

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:56.840
That changed everything, I would have never known,  I would have never know what that move did."

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:02.240
In his earliest days of competition, Mang0 made his name on Jigglypuff and innovated

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:04.340
much of her early playstyle.

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:10.900
But by the time Hungrybox was rising to prominence in the early 2010s, Jigglypuff’s star was

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:11.800
fading.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:17.560
Fox and Falco’s dominance in that era of Melee was a nightmare for ‘Puff players,

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:24.240
who died very quickly to their powerful upwards KO moves and had difficulty dealing with projectiles.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.920
But Hungrybox persevered.

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:32.760
Playing Jigglypuff runs contrary to just about everything a casual spectator knows about

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:33.760
Melee.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:39.620
She’s not fast, her combos tend to be rather short, and she’s extremely floaty, making

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:40.890
her easier to kill.

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:43.740
But she’s not without her strengths.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:48.520
That Rest kill that Hungrybox learned about as a kid is at the very center of what makes

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:50.060
Puff so powerful.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:56.320
but until she finds the hit, you have to play extremely patiently, working to get your opening.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:00.760
That level of patience has grown to define Hungrybox.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.460
"I would say Hungrybox has very very good patience."

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:05.640
"Yup."

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:08.740
"He has a gamestyle, and he's never forfeiting that gamestyle."

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:10.700
"At any cost pretty much."

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:17.240
"He's not going for risky stuff, he's going for whatever he thinks is the most practical option a lot of times."

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:21.580
"You kinda know what he's going to do but what he's doing is very safe so it's like ugh.

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:24.000
You see it coming but it's like I can't really do much about it right now

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.080
So it's all about picking your spots with him.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:29.840
And him he's kind of more of an annoying player just the way he plays.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:33.700
And if you start getting annoyed by it that's when he starts shining, that's when he gets in your head."

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:38.240
Already known as one of the best in the world, Hungrybox, finally pulled out of his slump

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:43.760
in 2013 and won the NorCal Regionals, beating Mango in the Grand Finals.

00:09:43.760 --> 00:10:01.080
(Casting)

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:07.180
He followed that up with a shocking third place finish at Evo 2013, which will go down

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:11.120
in history as one of Melee’s most stacked tournaments ever.

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:13.160
There he beat SFAT

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:22.660
(Casting)

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:23.440
Leffen.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:32.660
(Casting)

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:33.820
And Mew2King.

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:45.100
(Casting)

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:50.060
In fact, Hbox only lost to Wobbles and Mango, the two players who

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:52.580
faced off in the Grand Finals.

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:57.380
Hbox continued to display strong form for the next few years, placing second at the

00:10:57.440 --> 00:10:58.720
Big House 3

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:09.440
(Casting)

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:11.340
Third at CEO 2014

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:20.080
(Casting)

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.380
Second at Evo 2014

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:37.460
(Casting)

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:41.760
And finally, first place at Paragon Orlando 2015.

00:11:42.220 --> 00:12:01.200
(Casting)

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:32.400
(Casting)

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:37.540
There is something of a gap between Juan Debiedma and Hungrybox.

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:42.660
Throughout 2015 Hungrybox was arrogant, brash, and cocksure.

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:48.520
He knew he was one of the best, and he wasn’t afraid to play the heel to a crowd full of haters.

00:12:48.520 --> 00:13:03.440
(Crowd boos)

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:07.840
The patient playstyle that Hungrybox developed to make the most of Jigglypuff’s strong

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:13.220
kill option and her slow, floaty movement meant walling opponents out.

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:18.460
Which meant playing slow games that often had long periods of movement with no real action.

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:23.060
Which meant people weren’t exactly rushing to watch Hungrybox play.

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:26.620
But the community’s animosity spread further than that.

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:32.200
Hungrybox’s slow playstyle, combined with his over-the-top heel persona led to plenty

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:34.420
of hate from the Smash community.

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:40.520
And as the hatred grew, it began to make Hungrybox question if he should continue playing.

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:45.200
But, like all things in his life, his mom found a solution.

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:49.860
"I wanted to quit a lot of times, especially when I was like in that weird transition phase when I was trying to figure

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:53.520
out who I am. Between the ages of 18 and 21 I was like,

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:56.520
"Man, so many people hate me.

00:13:56.520 --> 00:13:59.400
Why am I doing this if people hate me I'm not getting any satisfaction from this"

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:04.240
And I was really gonna quit. But I think Luis and my mom talked me out of it."

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:09.470
Hungrybox didn’t just rub the community the wrong way at times, he also tended to

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:11.839
turn some pros against him as well.

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:18.600
Hungrybox and Mango have clashed several times in-game, but after Mango’s Evo 2013, win,

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:23.660
their rivalry spilled out into the internet, where the two traded barbs on Reddit.

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:28.820
But it’s his relationship with Leffen that has remained somewhat heated.

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:31.280
"All your sh*t talking, get f*cked Leffen.

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:37.000
I don't care, who ended up winning Leffen. Where are you now? Where are you now?"

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:39.940
"I wanna kick that guy's a**."

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:43.460
"I would be lying if I you know, didn't really wanna f*cking beat his a**."

00:14:43.460 --> 00:14:48.720
"And I want to make sure that when I do it, he remembers what happened to him at Summit."

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:52.440
"Last time he talked sh*t. And got blown up."

00:14:52.980 --> 00:15:13.440
(Casting)

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:18.680
Hungrybox spent much of 2015 without a significant win after Paragon Orlando.

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:23.500
He was winning smaller events, but when there was more than one other god in attendance,

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:27.140
Hungrybox was taking second, third, even fifth.

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:32.160
But he finally rounded the corner on the last stretch on his road to the top, when he won

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:34.600
DreamHack Winter 2015.

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:55.400
(Casting)

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:58.640
And after his victory, he broke down.

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:04.280
His father, who had told him he’d never amount to anything and left his family behind

00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:08.960
was gone, and Hungrybox was finally on his way to the top.

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:18.460
"D1, I've wanted this for so long. And you know my dad just passed away. It was my biological dad and we

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:23.620
I know this is personal stuff but he told me, "You'll never be the best, you'll be good but you'll never be the best"

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:26.180
So I hope he sees me now."

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:31.960
Despite a small slip at Genesis 3, 2016 was a banner year for Hbox.

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:41.700
First at Battle of the Five Gods, Pound 2016, EGLX and CEO 2016 put Hungrybox in the conversation

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:43.720
for the best player in the world.

00:16:46.020 --> 00:16:48.480
And then came Evo.

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:55.260
All through 2016, there was really only one person who could challenge Hungrybox: Armada.

00:16:55.260 --> 00:17:01.100
Armada was Europe’s best, and by most accounts, the greatest Melee player in the world.

00:17:01.100 --> 00:17:06.540
But Hungrybox was learning how to beat him, thanks in part to his coach, Crunch.

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:12.860
Crunch was a longtime friend, and was even one of the people that got him into competitive Melee.

00:17:12.860 --> 00:17:15.700
"We thought we were hot sh*t. We thought we could like beat anyone and we were like

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:17.700
Yeah we beat all of our friends, we're great at this game.

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:20.540
We watched like tournament videos, we're like "You know what these guys are good but we can probably

00:17:20.540 --> 00:17:21.580
be as good as them."

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:25.120
So that's kinda where it all started."

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:30.220
But Crunch was also the analytical mind behind Hungrybox’s success.

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:35.220
He helped him put together the strategies he needed to bring down players like Armada.

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:41.000
He was also the guy who helped Hbox translate that patience he learned from his family life

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:42.240
to his playstyle.

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:46.160
"Well here in person like right before a match it's all about mentality and warming up

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:51.020
making sure that all the stuff we worked on is fresh that he's ready to go into the match.

00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:54.940
Outside of that it's really like going into the lab like figuring out new strategies

00:17:54.940 --> 00:17:57.020
making sure everything's really really clean.

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:02.900
And going over his opponents and analyzing the matches to make sure we have a good strategy

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:03.700
going into the matches."

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:09.820
If DreamHack Winter was a coming out party for Hbox’s optimal playstyle, then Evo was

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:13.720
where he showed off exactly how optimal it was.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:19:06.440
(Casting)

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:10.240
"It's like we're back to 2015 with almost every grand finals is Armada Hungrybox."

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:13.420
"Here we are we're gonna see them duke it out one more time."

00:19:14.140 --> 00:19:19.200
Hbox drew first blood, taking the first two games back-to-back.

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:25.300
But Armada struck back with two games of his own with everything on the line, Hungrybox

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:30.560
clutched it out to reset the bracket with the Rest heard around the world.

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:48.960
(Casting)

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:52.800
This time the two traded games back and forth

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:55.200
But Hungrybox closed it out.

00:19:55.840 --> 00:20:11.680
(Casting)

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:18.860
The emotion of winning the biggest Melee tournament ever, with all eyes on him, overwhelmed him.

00:20:19.220 --> 00:20:21.520
He fell to the floor and cried.

00:20:22.040 --> 00:20:23.920
He was finally the best.

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:47.200
A few months after Evo 2016, Hungrybox quit his full-time job to focus on Smash.

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:53.360
"I've quit my job at Westrock. I've quit my job at Westrock.

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:55.760
And I'm gonna play Melee for the next few years.

00:20:56.040 --> 00:21:00.980
I'm gonna travel around the world, and I'm gonna visit all of my fans at tournaments

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:11.580
For years, Hungrybox had been balancing Smash and school, and then Smash and his engineering job.

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:18.300
But after years of waiting, Smash was finally big enough to support him the way he needed.

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:21.940
Once again, his patience was rewarded.

00:21:21.940 --> 00:21:26.780
Armada and Hungrybox went back and forth for the rest of the year, even agreeing that the

00:21:26.800 --> 00:21:30.320
winner of Smash Summit 3 would be the best player in the world.

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:47.280
(Casting)

00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:51.240
But 2017 was when Hbox shined.

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:53.920
First at DreamHack Austin

00:21:53.920 --> 00:22:04.000
(Casting)

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:05.700
CEO 2017,

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:15.080
(Casting)

00:22:15.080 --> 00:22:18.280
And perhaps most notably, Smash Summit 5,

00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:21.440
where he broke Armada’s 4-Summit streak.

00:22:21.720 --> 00:22:48.400
(Casting)

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:52.000
All that together finally put Hungrybox on top.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:56.980
He moved from second to first in the world rankings, pushing Armada off the top for the

00:22:56.980 --> 00:22:59.700
first time since 2015.

00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:05.820
And he’s remained strong, attending a tournament nearly every week in 2018, and either winning

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:08.140
